 # Hash Grid Loss
-Also called: Binary Hash Grid Entropy Loss
 ## Overview
-Hash Grid Loss is a regularization term used in compressive neural volume rendering to minimize the bit consumption of the binary occupancy structure of a hash grid during training [S1]. It operates by modeling the probability distribution of the binary hash grid values and optimizing the negative log-likelihood, effectively driving the model to create a more concentrated (sparse) hash grid structure [S1]. It is distinct from sibling methods like Voxel World Data Structure which manage spatial indexing, and from Entropy Loss which focuses on the continuous or quantized attribute values stored within active cells rather than the occupancy bits themselves [S1, 1101-voxel-world-data-structure.md].
